<title>SOUTH AFRICA: S.Africa port staff impound European beef shipment.</title>
<headline>S.Africa port staff impound European beef shipment.</headline>
<dateline>DURBAN, South Africa 1996-11-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>South African port officials have seized a consignment of 200 tonnes of imported beef because it included British stocks in violation of a ban, a government official said on Friday.</p>
<p>Agriculture department official Gideon Bruckner said 12 container-loads of beef from Europe were confiscated in the Indian Ocean port of Durban on October 25.</p>
<p>The consignment breached a ban on British beef exports sparked by the scare over Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE), also known as &quot;mad cow&quot; disease.</p>
<p>BSE is a brain-wasting disease that scientists say might be linked to a human equivalent Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease.</p>
<p>&quot;From our investigations we have found that when the shipment was still in Holland, stocks of British beef were added through re-packaging,&quot; Bruckner told Reuters.</p>
<p>South Africa had asked European Commission officials to probe the &quot;apparent irregularities&quot;, he said.</p>
</text>
